---
import BlogPostLayout from "../../layouts/BlogPostLayout.astro";
import BlogPhoto from "../../components/BlogPhoto.astro";
import { getBlogPost } from "../../data/blogPosts.js";

const post = getBlogPost("power-100-list");
---
<BlogPostLayout
  title={post.title}
  description={post.description}
  eyebrow="Mindset"
  dek={post.dek}
  date={post.date}
  readTime={post.readTime}
  image={post.image}
  isoMonth={post.isoMonth}
  faqs={post.faqs}
  ctaHeading="Want to talk through yours?"
  ctaBody="We get into stuff like this on the podcast all the time — the mindset side of fatherhood, not just the logistics. Come listen, or bring your list to the Spit-Up Society."
  ctaHref="/podcast"
  ctaLabel="Listen to the podcast"
>
  <p>
    A few months back I did an exercise from a book called <em>Wild Success</em>, by Calvin
    Coyles. It's built around a scorecard — you rate yourself across a handful of categories,
    and it spits out a number that's supposed to tell you how "wild" your success actually is.
    Mine came back at 35%. Not great. But the number wasn't the part that stuck with me.
  </p>

  <p>
    The part that stuck with me was a much simpler exercise buried a few chapters in: write down
    100 things you're proud of. Not 100 things you own. Not 100 things you're good at on a
    resume. 100 real things — moments, skills, decisions, scars — that you'd stand behind if
    someone asked you to defend your life so far.
  </p>

  <BlogPhoto src="/images/blog/dad-driving-phone.jpg" alt="A phone mounted on a car dashboard, playing music while driving" ratio="16/9" />

  <h2>Why 100, and why out loud</h2>
  <p>
    Here's the trick: don't sit down and type it. Talk it out loud, ideally somewhere you can't
    easily stop — driving is perfect. The first 20 come fast and they're the obvious ones. The
    next 30 get harder and force you to dig. By the last 20, you're pulling up stuff you forgot
    you'd done — and that's where the exercise actually starts working, because you stop
    performing for an imaginary audience and start just telling yourself the truth.
  </p>
  <p>
    I did mine in the car over a couple of drives. It felt a little ridiculous at first, talking
    to myself in traffic. Then it didn't.
  </p>

  <h2>What showed up on mine</h2>
  <p>
    I'm not going to give you all 100 — some of it's nobody's business but mine and Viv's. But
    here's a slice, unedited, in no particular order, to show you what "real" looks like on this
    list. It's not a highlight reel of achievements. It's a mix:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Being a dad</li>
    <li>Launching Dudela with Michael</li>
    <li>Our marriage's resilience — the actual hard years, not just the good ones</li>
    <li>Built the van — taught myself basics of electrical, plumbing, basic solar, drywall</li>
    <li>Been to 50 states and 10 countries</li>
    <li>Overcame a serious injury to become comeback athlete of the year in college</li>
    <li>Wrote a children's book for my girls</li>
    <li>Read music and play an instrument</li>
    <li>Renovated a house with my own hands</li>
    <li>Ran a half marathon</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="pull-quote">
    None of that is a brag list. It's a proof list — proof that you've already done hard, real
    things, on days when it doesn't feel like you're doing anything at all.
  </div>

  <BlogPhoto src="/images/blog/notebook-coffee.jpg" alt="A to-do list notepad next to a cup of coffee" ratio="4/5" position="left center" />

  <h2>The part that actually matters</h2>
  <p>
    Once the list exists, don't just file it away. Read back through it and look for patterns.
    Mine skewed heavily toward physical challenges, travel, and building things with my hands —
    and almost nothing about money or career titles. That told me something true about where I
    actually feel alive, versus where I'd been spending most of my mental energy. That gap is
    worth sitting with.
  </p>
  <p>
    It also does something simpler, on the bad days: it's evidence. Fatherhood has a way of
    making you feel like you're constantly behind, constantly failing at something. A list like
    this doesn't argue with that feeling — it just sits there as proof that the person having
    the bad day has also done a hundred things worth being proud of. Some days that's the whole
    difference.
  </p>

  <h2>Try it yourself</h2>
  <p>
    You don't need the book to do this part. Next time you're driving somewhere alone — school
    pickup, the grocery run, whatever — just start talking. Don't edit, don't rank, don't stop
    to fact-check yourself. Get to 100. It'll take a few drives. It's worth every one of them.
  </p>
</BlogPostLayout>
